WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.010 --> 00:00:03.370
<v Maya>New AI‑native CRM challenges giants.

2
00:00:03.370 --> 00:00:05.880
<v Maya>That, and fifteen more stories, on First Tick.

3
00:00:15.330 --> 00:00:17.020
<v Alex>This is Bits and Tickers.

4
00:00:17.020 --> 00:00:18.760
<v Alex>What got built, and what it's worth.

5
00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:19.160
<v Alex>I'm Alex.

6
00:00:19.770 --> 00:00:21.040
<v Maya>And I'm Maya.

7
00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:25.360
<v Maya>It's Monday, November 16th, and this is First Tick: the day's tech,

8
00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:30.000
<v Maya>AI and market news in about half an hour, with why each story matters.

9
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.290
<v Maya>Everything we talk about is linked in the show notes.

10
00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:34.420
<v Alex>On today's show.

11
00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:37.640
<v Alex>New AI‑native CRM challenges giants.

12
00:00:37.640 --> 00:00:40.540
<v Alex>Uber and Zoox hit the streets together.

13
00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:43.550
<v Alex>Chips to China spark congressional fire.

14
00:00:43.550 --> 00:00:46.420
<v Alex>Space launch disaster rocks ULA plans.

15
00:00:46.420 --> 00:00:50.100
<v Alex>And Musk pushes for Nvidia CEO return.

16
00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:51.460
<v Alex>Plus eleven more.

17
00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:52.210
<v Alex>Let's get into it.

18
00:00:53.750 --> 00:00:55.380
<v Maya>Time for The Ticker.

19
00:00:55.380 --> 00:01:03.540
<v Maya>At Friday's close, the S&amp;P 500 finished at 7,562, down 0.4 percent.

20
00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:09.300
<v Maya>The Nasdaq Composite was down 0.9 percent and the Dow was up 0.1 percent.

21
00:01:09.590 --> 00:01:13.480
<v Alex>And in big tech, Meta had the best day, up 2.9 percent,

22
00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:18.090
<v Alex>while Nvidia was at the other end, down 2.0 percent.

23
00:01:18.090 --> 00:01:19.760
<v Alex>That's a snapshot, not advice.

24
00:01:21.460 --> 00:01:24.440
<v Maya>They’re targeting Salesforce and HubSpot, says TechCrunch.

25
00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:25.140
<v Alex>That fast?

26
00:01:25.660 --> 00:01:29.000
<v Maya>The seed and Series A were led by Together Fund and Accel,

27
00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:30.380
<v Maya>with Premji Invest in the mix.

28
00:01:30.940 --> 00:01:34.670
<v Alex>Dreamteam says it auto‑captures email and call data,

29
00:01:34.670 --> 00:01:39.110
<v Alex>then uses four AI agents to draft follow‑ups and monitor pipelines.

30
00:01:39.790 --> 00:01:42.690
<v Maya>They’ve got over 100 customers in three months,

31
00:01:42.690 --> 00:01:45.660
<v Maya>and some have swapped Salesforce or HubSpot entirely.

32
00:01:46.540 --> 00:01:52.590
<v Alex>A16z warned that AI will sit on top of existing CRMs, not replace them.

33
00:01:52.940 --> 00:01:57.120
<v Maya>So the startup’s own CEO says the market is still open for a rethink,

34
00:01:57.120 --> 00:01:58.320
<v Maya>not just feature add‑ons.

35
00:01:58.520 --> 00:02:01.460
<v Maya>What actually powers that Company Brain?

36
00:02:01.460 --> 00:02:03.540
<v Maya>How does it pull data from emails and calls?

37
00:02:03.940 --> 00:02:06.220
<v Alex>It stitches together raw logs,

38
00:02:06.220 --> 00:02:11.560
<v Alex>then feeds them into four specialized agents that can draft emails, flag risks,

39
00:02:11.560 --> 00:02:12.500
<v Alex>and tweak workflows.

40
00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:16.280
<v Maya>Those agents—are they all separate models or one monolith?

41
00:02:16.660 --> 00:02:19.760
<v Alex>Four distinct ones, each tuned to a task,

42
00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:22.460
<v Alex>but all sharing the same underlying memory pool.

43
00:02:22.942 --> 00:02:24.460
<v Maya>What about the funding split?

44
00:02:24.460 --> 00:02:25.780
<v Maya>Did the seed and Series A differ?

45
00:02:26.260 --> 00:02:30.110
<v Alex>Seed led by Together Fund, Series A by Accel,

46
00:02:30.110 --> 00:02:35.080
<v Alex>with Premji Invest joining; the article keeps the exact amounts and valuation

47
00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:35.660
<v Alex>hidden.

48
00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:40.140
<v Maya>So no valuation reveal—does that hint at a cautious approach?

49
00:02:40.610 --> 00:02:42.160
<v Alex>Could be; they’re still early,

50
00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:45.540
<v Alex>and the market’s tight—maybe they’re keeping options open.

51
00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:46.600
<v Alex>Wow, that speed?

52
00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:51.460
<v Maya>Yeah, 100 customers in three months is nothing to sneeze at,

53
00:02:51.460 --> 00:02:53.100
<v Maya>but it’s a tiny slice of the market.

54
00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:53.840
<v Alex>Even so?

55
00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:58.000
<v Maya>True, but swapping a system of record at scale is a different beast.

56
00:02:58.540 --> 00:03:02.100
<v Alex>If they’re pulling data from emails, they need a solid pipeline.

57
00:03:02.740 --> 00:03:05.500
<v Maya>They say four agents share a memory pool,

58
00:03:05.500 --> 00:03:07.820
<v Maya>but stitching that together is no small task.

59
00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:11.840
<v Alex>I think the real win is automating follow‑ups and flagging risks.

60
00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:15.140
<v Maya>So the market’s still open, but incumbents will adapt.

61
00:03:16.820 --> 00:03:18.140
<v Maya>Did you see that?

62
00:03:18.140 --> 00:03:22.100
<v Maya>Zoox robotaxis are now in the Uber app in Las Vegas, TechCrunch reports.

63
00:03:22.710 --> 00:03:27.760
<v Alex>Yeah, it’s the first time Amazon‑owned Zoox teamed up with a ride‑hailing platform.

64
00:03:28.220 --> 00:03:32.090
<v Maya>And Uber’s pulling in a second driverless partner after Waymo,

65
00:03:32.090 --> 00:03:34.760
<v Maya>but they’re not giving Zoox the same maintenance support.

66
00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:38.860
<v Alex>So Uber keeps the muscle on the tech side and just drops the cars.

67
00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:41.860
<v Maya>They’re planning to keep it local for now,

68
00:03:41.860 --> 00:03:46.580
<v Maya>with a multi‑year tie at MGM Resorts to help with pickups and signage.

69
00:03:47.270 --> 00:03:51.480
<v Alex>I think that’s just a clever way to get a handle on the customer experience.

70
00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:57.900
<v Maya>The plan is to expand supply later and maybe add a dedicated autonomous option when

71
00:03:57.900 --> 00:03:59.240
<v Maya>reliability improves.

72
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:03.780
<v Alex>Sounds like Uber is testing the waters before fully committing,

73
00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:05.580
<v Alex>while Zoox gets a foot in the door.

74
00:04:06.100 --> 00:04:07.640
<v Alex>One thousand robotaxis?

75
00:04:07.640 --> 00:04:08.020
<v Alex>Seriously?

76
00:04:08.600 --> 00:04:13.630
<v Maya>Nevada Transportation Authority approved Uber to deploy up to that many Zoox units

77
00:04:13.630 --> 00:04:14.680
<v Maya>in Clark County.

78
00:04:14.772 --> 00:04:16.200
<v Alex>No direct selection yet.

79
00:04:16.580 --> 00:04:20.600
<v Maya>Customers can tweak ride preferences to bump odds of getting a Zoox.

80
00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:21.360
<v Alex>Hidden mode?

81
00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:26.860
<v Maya>Uber plans a dedicated autonomous option once reliability improves,

82
00:04:26.860 --> 00:04:27.900
<v Maya>maybe later this year.

83
00:04:28.040 --> 00:04:28.780
<v Alex>MGM signage?

84
00:04:29.310 --> 00:04:34.560
<v Maya>MGM Resorts will manage pickup zones and signage for Zoox robotaxis in their

85
00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:34.960
<v Maya>properties.

86
00:04:35.500 --> 00:04:39.940
<v Maya>So Uber is basically letting Zoox ride on their platform without maintenance—kind

87
00:04:39.940 --> 00:04:40.680
<v Maya>of a free ride?

88
00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:41.980
<v Alex>It's a test run.

89
00:04:41.980 --> 00:04:44.600
<v Alex>They’re not putting their tech in the driver's seat.

90
00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:49.640
<v Maya>But they’re still signing a multi-year deal with MGM; that feels like a strategic

91
00:04:49.640 --> 00:04:50.160
<v Maya>foothold.

92
00:04:50.700 --> 00:04:54.260
<v Alex>Maybe they just want the brand buzz, not the engineering headaches.

93
00:04:54.980 --> 00:05:00.100
<v Maya>I think the real win is for Uber to own more autonomous supply before Waymo drops

94
00:05:00.100 --> 00:05:00.500
<v Maya>out.

95
00:05:01.020 --> 00:05:01.920
<v Alex>I’m not convinced.

96
00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:05.280
<v Alex>If reliability lags, customers will get frustrated.

97
00:05:06.190 --> 00:05:10.910
<v Maya>Still, having a dedicated autonomous option eventually could tidy up the app.

98
00:05:11.750 --> 00:05:14.080
<v Alex>It sounds like a cautious rollout,

99
00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:17.930
<v Alex>but the lack of maintenance support makes me wonder how long they’ll stick around.

100
00:05:19.600 --> 00:05:22.840
<v Alex>Maya, did you hear the latest from The Verge about Nvidia chips?

101
00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:24.780
<v Maya>Yeah, she fired the secretary.

102
00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:28.100
<v Alex>She claims national security was undermined.

103
00:05:28.820 --> 00:05:30.500
<v Maya>Companies tied to China got chips.

104
00:05:30.740 --> 00:05:31.680
<v Alex>No way, big claim.

105
00:05:32.500 --> 00:05:35.020
<v Maya>Letter cites clear congressional authority ignored.

106
00:05:35.840 --> 00:05:37.280
<v Alex>Is the secretary being accused?

107
00:05:37.730 --> 00:05:39.440
<v Maya>Letter sent Sunday, says The Verge.

108
00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:41.960
<v Alex>We’ll unpack its supply chain impact.

109
00:05:42.940 --> 00:05:44.320
<v Maya>What’s the core of the letter’s claim?

110
00:05:44.710 --> 00:05:49.960
<v Alex>It says the Commerce Department has let AI chips slip into China through firms that

111
00:05:49.960 --> 00:05:54.240
<v Alex>have business ties there, and that’s a breach of congressional authority.

112
00:05:54.540 --> 00:05:54.980
<v Maya>No way.

113
00:05:55.430 --> 00:05:59.820
<v Alex>The letter specifically calls that a “national security” issue,

114
00:05:59.820 --> 00:06:04.720
<v Alex>saying the flow of those chips “undermines America’s technology advantage.”

115
00:06:05.340 --> 00:06:06.870
<v Maya>So the Secretary’s name is in it?

116
00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:11.800
<v Alex>Yes, the letter is addressed to Secretary Howard Lutnick and accuses him of

117
00:06:11.800 --> 00:06:15.620
<v Alex>neglecting the rules that are supposed to keep the chips out of China.

118
00:06:15.700 --> 00:06:16.800
<v Maya>Any counter from the DOC?

119
00:06:17.340 --> 00:06:21.640
<v Alex>No response yet, the article only reports the Senator’s accusations.

120
00:06:22.410 --> 00:06:26.420
<v Alex>So basically the DOC just handed over some chips and shrugged.

121
00:06:26.930 --> 00:06:29.860
<v Maya>The letter says they let them slip through firms tied to China.

122
00:06:30.100 --> 00:06:30.460
<v Alex>No way.

123
00:06:30.900 --> 00:06:33.720
<v Maya>The Senator thinks it's a national‑security hit,

124
00:06:33.720 --> 00:06:35.990
<v Maya>but the DOC hasn't even responded yet.

125
00:06:36.480 --> 00:06:38.320
<v Alex>If the rules are clear, why the delay?

126
00:06:38.720 --> 00:06:42.010
<v Maya>The letter says Congress gave the DOC the authority,

127
00:06:42.010 --> 00:06:43.700
<v Maya>so the Secretary’s in the hot seat.

128
00:06:44.500 --> 00:06:47.640
<v Alex>Maybe it’s just a bureaucratic slip, not a chess move.

129
00:06:48.061 --> 00:06:52.140
<v Maya>The point is, the Senator wants to see enforcement, not just paperwork.

130
00:06:53.910 --> 00:06:58.900
<v Maya>A Vulcan rocket fell back after launch, taking 40 Amazon satellites with it.

131
00:06:58.960 --> 00:07:00.060
<v Alex>That’s a pretty big hit.

132
00:07:00.460 --> 00:07:05.300
<v Maya>The rocket was supposed to launch half of the US military's upcoming space missions.

133
00:07:05.420 --> 00:07:06.240
<v Alex>Uh, so they’re grounded?

134
00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:09.760
<v Maya>Yes, for an indefinite period, according to the report.

135
00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:11.220
<v Alex>Sounds like a frequent fail.

136
00:07:11.840 --> 00:07:16.440
<v Maya>It’s not the first close call; previous boosters had issues but the rocket still

137
00:07:16.440 --> 00:07:17.100
<v Maya>reached orbit.

138
00:07:17.880 --> 00:07:19.900
<v Alex>So they’ll review it before flying again.

139
00:07:20.490 --> 00:07:24.310
<v Alex>How many of those Amazon satellites were in the payload, exactly?

140
00:07:24.640 --> 00:07:29.720
<v Maya>Forty, all part of a 38-launch contract to roll out Amazon’s broadband

141
00:07:29.720 --> 00:07:30.600
<v Maya>constellation.

142
00:07:31.180 --> 00:07:35.420
<v Alex>So they’re still slated to launch the rest, or is the schedule on hold?

143
00:07:35.720 --> 00:07:40.140
<v Maya>They’re on hold indefinitely; the failure triggers a full review before any flight.

144
00:07:40.225 --> 00:07:41.580
<v Alex>That review takes how long?

145
00:07:41.850 --> 00:07:46.700
<v Maya>Months, but it’s already started; ULA had two earlier booster issues and still

146
00:07:46.700 --> 00:07:48.500
<v Maya>launched on schedule after fixes.

147
00:07:49.020 --> 00:07:52.520
<v Alex>Did ULA say why they kept going after the booster hiccups?

148
00:07:52.840 --> 00:07:58.360
<v Maya>They mentioned “review and corrective actions” but no exact timeline; the report

149
00:07:58.360 --> 00:08:00.200
<v Maya>just says they’ll resume when ready.

150
00:08:00.740 --> 00:08:03.460
<v Maya>That review could drag on, months, maybe longer.

151
00:08:04.340 --> 00:08:06.200
<v Alex>Yeah, but ULA has bounced before.

152
00:08:06.670 --> 00:08:08.260
<v Maya>What about the $40 satellites?

153
00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:12.040
<v Alex>40 is just a number, but it's a 38‑launch deal.

154
00:08:12.460 --> 00:08:14.300
<v Maya>So the whole constellation is on hold.

155
00:08:14.800 --> 00:08:16.580
<v Alex>It sounds like a delay, not a halt.

156
00:08:17.060 --> 00:08:19.100
<v Maya>The military’s payloads might wait, too.

157
00:08:19.540 --> 00:08:23.600
<v Alex>I think the real issue is the engineering gap that keeps cropping up.

158
00:08:25.380 --> 00:08:30.840
<v Alex>Musk, Replit, Epic and Y Combinator all signed a petition for Nvidia's CEO back.

159
00:08:31.340 --> 00:08:35.980
<v Maya>They say Huang was removed without due process, and they want an independent probe.

160
00:08:36.160 --> 00:08:36.640
<v Alex>No way.

161
00:08:37.080 --> 00:08:41.840
<v Maya>Nvidia named CFO Colette Kress interim while they hunt a permanent replacement.

162
00:08:42.520 --> 00:08:45.160
<v Maya>It sounds like a big fan club rallying for a CEO.

163
00:08:45.780 --> 00:08:48.600
<v Alex>Hold on, is this just hype or genuine concern?

164
00:08:48.940 --> 00:08:53.560
<v Maya>According to TechCrunch, the petition demands a fair investigation and more public

165
00:08:53.560 --> 00:08:54.060
<v Maya>info.

166
00:08:54.480 --> 00:08:56.160
<v Maya>What’s still unclear for our listeners?

167
00:08:56.520 --> 00:08:59.820
<v Maya>Wait, did you say 5,965 employees?

168
00:09:00.500 --> 00:09:06.540
<v Alex>Yeah, and 1,108 former staff plus 63,063 shareholders.

169
00:09:07.280 --> 00:09:09.100
<v Maya>That’s a mix of insiders and investors.

170
00:09:09.720 --> 00:09:14.460
<v Alex>The petition says Huang was ousted without proper due process and wants an

171
00:09:14.460 --> 00:09:15.540
<v Alex>independent probe.

172
00:09:16.020 --> 00:09:17.640
<v Maya>So the board’s move is under fire.

173
00:09:18.050 --> 00:09:22.490
<v Alex>With CFO Colette Kress stepping in, the stock took a dip,

174
00:09:22.490 --> 00:09:25.400
<v Alex>and people worry the AI funding gap will widen.

175
00:09:25.910 --> 00:09:31.110
<v Alex>No way, a CEO rally for the board to reverse a decision that was already made.

176
00:09:31.620 --> 00:09:35.140
<v Maya>It sounds like a campaign to pressure the board to bring him back while an

177
00:09:35.140 --> 00:09:36.440
<v Maya>independent probe runs.

178
00:09:36.950 --> 00:09:42.200
<v Alex>Hold on, the petition says due process was missing, but that’s a political angle,

179
00:09:42.200 --> 00:09:43.290
<v Alex>not a technical one.

180
00:09:43.630 --> 00:09:47.770
<v Maya>The board had to act on governance; they didn’t ignore due process,

181
00:09:47.770 --> 00:09:49.360
<v Maya>they chose an interim CEO.

182
00:09:50.410 --> 00:09:57.270
<v Alex>But the numbers—5,965 employees, 1,108 ex‑staff,

183
00:09:57.270 --> 00:10:02.060
<v Alex>63,063 shareholders—show real internal support.

184
00:10:02.580 --> 00:10:06.320
<v Maya>That mix shows the board faces pressure from inside and outside,

185
00:10:06.320 --> 00:10:07.340
<v Maya>not just a fan club.

186
00:10:07.870 --> 00:10:13.220
<v Alex>So my read is that this is more about optics and stakeholder confidence than actual

187
00:10:13.220 --> 00:10:14.200
<v Alex>governance failure.

188
00:10:14.660 --> 00:10:17.800
<v Maya>It is a sign that the board needs to explain its steps,

189
00:10:17.800 --> 00:10:21.350
<v Maya>and the market will judge whether that transparency is enough.

190
00:10:23.160 --> 00:10:26.340
<v Maya>Nvidia's CSO left August, CEO quits too.

191
00:10:26.890 --> 00:10:28.600
<v Alex>Wait, two departures in a few months?

192
00:10:28.600 --> 00:10:29.200
<v Alex>That's…

193
00:10:29.200 --> 00:10:29.640
<v Alex>weird.

194
00:10:30.050 --> 00:10:32.400
<v Maya>Reber said health reasons, Parkinson's.

195
00:10:32.400 --> 00:10:33.170
<v Maya>He posted that.

196
00:10:33.590 --> 00:10:35.660
<v Alex>Health reason, then CEO…

197
00:10:35.660 --> 00:10:36.580
<v Alex>relationships now?

198
00:10:36.890 --> 00:10:40.160
<v Maya>Huang linked to Megaspeed, and employee affairs.

199
00:10:40.160 --> 00:10:41.350
<v Maya>TechCrunch cites reports.

200
00:10:42.280 --> 00:10:44.940
<v Alex>Does the security team know about Megaspeed?

201
00:10:44.940 --> 00:10:45.440
<v Alex>Unclear.

202
00:10:46.100 --> 00:10:49.820
<v Maya>Security teams often underfunded, says ex‑intelligence officer.

203
00:10:50.750 --> 00:10:52.930
<v Alex>Yeah, that means they might miss everyday leaks.

204
00:10:53.640 --> 00:10:56.840
<v Alex>What’s the real scoop on the new CSO, Saša Zdjelar?

205
00:10:57.300 --> 00:11:02.560
<v Maya>He’s a former ExxonMobil senior IT manager, moved to Salesforce,

206
00:11:02.560 --> 00:11:07.260
<v Maya>then ReversingLabs before Nvidia, but no word on his security briefings.

207
00:11:07.920 --> 00:11:10.200
<v Alex>So no link to Megaspeed or Huang’s affairs?

208
00:11:10.780 --> 00:11:12.100
<v Maya>None surfaced.

209
00:11:12.100 --> 00:11:15.740
<v Maya>The only public note is that the lawyer was in talks with the government over

210
00:11:15.740 --> 00:11:17.120
<v Maya>Megaspeed a year ago.

211
00:11:17.220 --> 00:11:18.440
<v Alex>Lawyer talks, not security?

212
00:11:19.060 --> 00:11:19.930
<v Maya>Exactly.

213
00:11:19.930 --> 00:11:24.100
<v Maya>The security team’s remit is narrow, and they get flagged as “compliance” only.

214
00:11:24.640 --> 00:11:27.160
<v Alex>That paint a picture of a bubble‑filled org chart.

215
00:11:27.550 --> 00:11:30.720
<v Maya>And the ex‑intelligence officer said they’re underfunded,

216
00:11:30.720 --> 00:11:32.460
<v Maya>so low‑level chatter slips by.

217
00:11:32.970 --> 00:11:35.340
<v Alex>So the leaks that happen every day just slip through?

218
00:11:35.680 --> 00:11:40.880
<v Maya>It sounds like Nvidia just completely dropped the ball on security, honestly.

219
00:11:40.880 --> 00:11:44.480
<v Maya>A lawyer talking to the government, but not the security team?

220
00:11:44.480 --> 00:11:45.430
<v Maya>That’s…

221
00:11:45.430 --> 00:11:45.900
<v Maya>concerning.

222
00:11:46.590 --> 00:11:47.920
<v Alex>Hold on, though.

223
00:11:47.920 --> 00:11:50.120
<v Alex>It's not necessarily a dropped ball.

224
00:11:50.120 --> 00:11:54.310
