225
00:11:54.310 --> 00:11:58.260
<v Alex>and that's what they meant when they said they regularly contact officials.

226
00:11:58.840 --> 00:12:03.000
<v Maya>Since when does “regular contact” with the government cover up personal

227
00:12:03.000 --> 00:12:05.320
<v Maya>relationships and potential chip smuggling?

228
00:12:05.760 --> 00:12:08.330
<v Maya>But if they should have been, Alex, that’s the problem.

229
00:12:08.330 --> 00:12:10.840
<v Maya>Underfunded, narrowly focused…

230
00:12:10.840 --> 00:12:12.680
<v Maya>that’s a recipe for missing the obvious.

231
00:12:13.280 --> 00:12:13.960
<v Alex>Maybe.

232
00:12:13.960 --> 00:12:17.960
<v Alex>But it’s also easy to armchair quarterback from the outside.

233
00:12:17.960 --> 00:12:22.980
<v Alex>It’s a huge company, with complex layers of reporting and information flow—things

234
00:12:22.980 --> 00:12:23.790
<v Alex>slip through.

235
00:12:24.260 --> 00:12:25.290
<v Maya>I suppose.

236
00:12:25.290 --> 00:12:28.100
<v Maya>But the optics are terrible, regardless.

237
00:12:28.100 --> 00:12:31.280
<v Maya>Two key departures, linked to serious issues…

238
00:12:31.280 --> 00:12:33.360
<v Maya>it doesn't reflect well on the board’s oversight.

239
00:12:33.950 --> 00:12:39.030
<v Alex>It paints a picture of a company prioritizing growth over everything else,

240
00:12:39.030 --> 00:12:43.400
<v Alex>and that’s a pattern you see in a lot of tech—it’s a fragile foundation.

241
00:12:45.040 --> 00:12:46.440
